<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Bucharest (1812)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1812">1812</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1806-1812">Russo-Turkish War (1806–1812)</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a>, signed on 28 May 1812, ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1806-1812">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1806–1812. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> the eastern half of the Principality of Moldavia between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prut">Prut</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a>, which became the Russian province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> gained limited autonomy. The peace freed Russian troops just before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s invasion of Russia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bucharest-1812.jpg" alt="Treaty of Bucharest (1812)" width="320" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexander Altenhof (KaterBegemot); derivative work by Durero · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Southeast_Europe_1812_map_en.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
